            "question_id": 13812,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 500,
            "sort": 2,
            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-YsEsdasoLo84dRx8xoRlvWJ5gA2\">Overall, while the Netherlands and Sweden showed an upward trend in illness absence rates, France experienced a significant decline. Meanwhile, the figures for the UK and Germany remained relatively stable throughout the period. In addition, the Netherlands consistently recorded the highest proportion of absent workers among all countries shown.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13812,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 501,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-FdTwd1aQro2Tjcx7E4llkbk5gTg\">Looking at the countries with noticeable changes, in 1991, both the Netherlands and France started at approximately 5% of workers taking sick leave, while Sweden's figure stood at around 3%. The Dutch rate then fluctuated before dropping to 4% in 1996, after which it rose steadily to reach about 5.5% by 2001. In contrast, the French percentage declined to roughly 3% then leveled off until the end of the period. Sweden, meanwhile, saw a gradual increase from 3% to 4.5% during the same timeframe.</div>\n</div>",
